{"agent":"impact","findings":[{"id":"impact-1","file":"triggarr/clients/base.py","line":232,"title":"Reverts security fix d47b4c2: raw HTTPStatusError logged again, which can expose credentials in the URL in stderr and the in-UI log viewer","category":"blast-radius","cwe":"CWE-532","severity":"medium","agent_confidence":85,"in_diff":true,"intent_doc_match":false,"problem":"This hunk exactly reverses commit d47b4c2 ('fix(security): prevent potential key leakage in validate_connection log messages'). That commit replaced `exc=exc` with `status=exc.response.status_code`. str(httpx.HTTPStatusError) has the form "Client error '403 Forbidden' for url '&lt;full request.url&gt;'", and httpx's URL __str__ keeps the userinfo and query string (only repr masks the password). The redacting sink (triggarr/logging.py:20-41) only replaces exact values returned by collect_secrets (triggarr/startup.py:75-100): the *arr api_key fields and the auth secrets. Two things in the URL fall outside that list. (a) Reverse-proxy basic-auth userinfo in the configured base_url, e.g. http://user:pass@host/sonarr. The URL validators in models/config.py only reject apikey= query keys and allow userinfo. (b) An apikey= value that differs from the configured api_key field. Both now reach stderr and log_buffer, and log_buffer feeds the in-UI log viewer. The rest of the codebase sanitizes this same exception class on purpose: engine._sanitize_exc (engine.py:30-44) returns only 'HTTP &lt;status&gt;', and the CR-01 comments at scheduler.py:218-224, scheduler.py:400-404 and routes.py:957-962 apply it to every httpx/pydantic exception. _request_with_retry in this same file (base.py:71-75) also logs only the status code. This diff makes validate_connection the one httpx log path that skips that discipline. Trigger: a startup validate_connection (startup.py:160) that gets any non-401 status, such as a 403 or 502 from a reverse proxy.","current_code":"logger.warning(
    "{app}: Unexpected HTTP error: {exc}",
    app=self._app_name,
    exc=exc,
)","fix_hint":"Restore status=exc.response.status_code, or call engine._sanitize_exc(exc), which is the established CR-01 pattern.","why_it_matters":"It removes a control that was added as a dedicated security fix. Credentials that the redactor does not know about become readable by anyone who can see container logs or the web log viewer.","silenced_marker_nearby":false},{"id":"impact-2","file":"triggarr/clients/base.py","line":251,"title":"Reverts sanitization of pydantic.ValidationError: response body values now logged verbatim (data exposure / log injection)","category":"blast-radius","cwe":"CWE-532","severity":"low","agent_confidence":70,"in_diff":true,"intent_doc_match":false,"problem":"This is the second half of the d47b4c2 revert. str(pydantic.ValidationError) includes `input_value=` excerpts of the data that failed validation. Here that data is the /system/status response body, which may come from a misconfigured reverse proxy, a captive portal or some other service at the configured URL. That body is untrusted, unbounded in content (pydantic truncates each value but lists every error) and multi-line, and it now flows into stderr and the in-UI log buffer. engine._sanitize_exc (engine.py:42-43) deliberately turns this exception type into 'validation error (N issues)', and the scheduler and routes CR-01 paths call it for pydantic errors. Severity is low because SystemStatus payloads from a real *arr instance rarely carry secrets. The defect is that a deliberate sanitization was removed with no replacement.","current_code":"logger.warning(
    "{app}: Unexpected API response format: {exc}",
    app=self._app_name,
    exc=exc,
)","fix_hint":"Restore count=exc.error_count(), or use _sanitize_exc(exc).","why_it_matters":"It reopens a log-content path that the project sanitizes everywhere else and that its own CLAUDE.md Security rules forbid (no internal error details or sensitive data in logs).","silenced_marker_nearby":false}],"agent_notes":["The diff is a byte-for-byte revert of commit d47b4c2, 'fix(security): prevent potential key leakage in validate_connection log messages'. Git history (git log -L on base.py:225-256) shows the pre-d47b4c2 text is identical to the new text. No same-purpose replacement exists: validate_connection does not route through _sanitize_exc, and the only other guard is the redacting sink, which covers a narrower property (exact known secret values only).","Blast radius: validate_connection is only called from startup.validate_connections (startup.py:160), once per configured instance at boot. It is inherited by RadarrClient, SonarrClient and LidarrClient (Lidarr overrides only _status_path). No public signature, return type, schema or persisted-data change. The 10 importers of base.py are unaffected functionally.","Exposure surface: log output goes to stderr (Docker logs) and to log_buffer, which the web UI log viewer reads (see backlog item 999.3/999.6 about log-viewer leaks). Any authenticated UI user, or anyone with docker logs access, sees the new content.","Consistency: this reintroduces the exact pattern that the CR-01 sanitization split (scheduler.py:218-224, scheduler.py:400-404, routes.py:957-962) and base.py:_request_with_retry (base.py:71-75) were written to eliminate. ROADMAP backlog 999.6 ('Close secret-redaction gaps across all log paths') goes the opposite direction from this diff.","Tests: tests/test_clients.py covers validate_connection return values (401, 403, connect, timeout, DNS, SSL) but asserts no log message content for the non-401 HTTP branch or the ValidationError branch, so the suite will pass and will not catch this regression. A caplog assertion that the URL and input_value are absent would lock it in.","Performance: none.","Verdict: Blocking issue. This undoes a dedicated security fix with no replacement; restore the status-code / error-count logging or use _sanitize_exc."]}

